Sigma Membership Pi Epsilon Scholarships and Research Grants Pre-Licensure Fees Preceptor Demographic Form Nurse Practitioner Preceptors Toggle menu Sign Up to Be a Nurse Practitioner Preceptor Preceptor Orientation Licensure Requirements Toggle menu Professional Pre-Licensure Graduate Nursing Licensure Community Directed Spending: Regis College Transition to Nurse Practice Technical Requirements for Pre-licensure Program Nursing Students Theresa Wood Lavine Professional Studies Programs Toggle menu Professional Studies Certificates Toggle menu Health Care Professional Certificates Professional Skills Certificates IT Professional Certificates Bachelors’ Completion Scholarships for Professional Certificate Holders Regis College Workforce Development Council Toggle menu Resources Fast Track Programs Toggle menu Three-Year Bachelor's Degree Toggle menu Frequently Asked Questions Combined Bachelor's/Master's Degree Special Programs Toggle menu NCLEX Review Program Honors Program Learning Beyond the Classroom Toggle menu Co-Curricular Courses Co-Curricular Mini Grants Regis In Haiti Summer Scholars Program Toggle menu Summer Scholars Frequently Asked Questions Writing Program Learning on a Global Scale Toggle menu Eligibility and Requirements Resources Cross Registration Lorraine's Leaders Toggle menu Application Research at Regis Toggle menu Research Grants and Contracts Intersession Classes Toggle menu Winter Intersession Summer Intersession Intersession Registration Global Experiences Toggle menu Sustainable Community Wellness and Nutrition in the Americas Internships and Career Development Toggle menu Career Assessment Tools Toggle menu How to Find an Internship Alumni Resources Recent Internships Graduate Career Services Handshake: Online Job Board The Flatley Scholars Program Library Toggle menu Library Services Toggle menu Library Hours Interlibrary Loans Toggle menu Requesting Books Requesting Articles Minuteman Library Network Borrowing Policies Course Reserve Materials Copyright Policy Toggle menu HEOA P2P File Sharing Requirements The Pearl Nelson Education Resource Center Library Team Registrar and Academic Records Toggle menu Withdrawal Process Degree Audit FAQ Final Exam Schedule Registrar Team Frequently Asked Questions Academic Credit FERPA Confidentiality Transcripts and Verifications Diploma Replacement Academic Integrity Students Right To Know ​Academic Support and Advising Toggle menu Academic Advising Toggle menu Undergraduate Academic Advising Graduate and Professional Student Academic Advising Advising Forms Eight-Semester Plans FAQs and Policies Important Resources Transfer Student Advising Your Advising Experience Institute for Learning Toggle menu Staff Student Support Coaching Tutoring FAQ Writing Support Services ​Lifelong Learning at Regis (LLARC) Toggle menu Study Groups Enrollment Information Membership and Tuition Information Request Information Lunch, Listen and Learn Speaker Series Brady-Griffith Scholarship Fund LLARC Committee Members Accreditation and State Regulatory Authorizations Toggle menu NECHE Self-Study Online Feedback Forms Professional Licensure Disclosures We are dedicated to guiding Regis College students through a transformative career development process, connecting academic excellence with experiential learning opportunities. Our mission is to launch compassionate leaders and skilled professionals who contribute directly to the vitality and well-being of local and global communities. For every college student, selecting a major is critical to finding the path right for them after graduation. At Regis, we let your passions be the guide and offer expert guidance to find the career that is best for you. 95% of 2025 graduates who responded to a survey employed and/or enrolled in graduate school within six months of graduation 97% of Regis students were employed and/or enrolled in graduate school within six months of graduation (five-year average) 100% Regis College students complete at least one internship and/or clinical during their educational experience With access to internships at some of the world's most cutting-edge and innovative companies, the university's Center for Internships and Career Development works to ensure that your college career provides you with the best first-hand experience to discover your purpose. Assistance is provided throughout your college experience. The center: Assists you in finding a career relevant to your major Helps you to find critical internships prior to graduation (every student is required to complete one internship to receive their degree) Connects you to an online career development program beginning your first year Produces a professional development seminar and several recruiting events throughout the academic year Offers one-on-one counseling The Flatley Scholars Program The Flatley Scholars Program is designed to promote career development for undergraduate students. The program is designed to provide stipends to undergraduate students with demonstrated financial needs who have secured unpaid, off-campus internships. Center for Internships and Career Development Four-Year Career Plan Year One: Explore Meet with the Center for Internships and Career Development to discuss future goals. Complete a skills assessment. Log in to your Handshake account for access to jobs, internship opportunities, and valuable career tools. Get involved with campus activities, clubs, and community service programs. Create your base resume to begin your job search. Set up an appointment with the center to identify career options. Year Two: Discover Meet with the Center for Internships and Career Development to clarify career plans. Build your online brand by creating a LinkedIn account and ensure social media accounts are appropriate and professional. Practice introducing yourself and making connections by attending social events on campus. Seek leadership positions in clubs, athletics, and college and community organizations. Attend career and professional development opportunities on campus and in the community. Year Three: Connect Gather information on summer learning opportunities like study abroad and community projects. Secure an internship and keep track of your accomplishments. Schedule a meeting for resume review. If attending graduate school, start researching admission requirements and preparing for standardized tests (GRE, LSAT, MCAT, etc.). Build practical experience through academic research and class projects. Begin asking faculty and professional staff for permission to use them as a professional reference. Year Four: Achieve Polish your resume and have it reviewed by the Center for Internships and Career Development. Check Handshake and other job boards (LinkedIn, Indeed.com, etc.) regularly for professional job postings. Make an appointment for a mock interview with the Center for Internships and Career Development or practice interviewing with a visiting employer, friends, or family. Gather information and contacts by attending Job Fairs and Workshops by building your professional network through LinkedIn. Complete applications for graduate or professional schools early. Regis College Footer menu SPIKE - Student Portal SHARP - Faculty/Staff Portal Weather Emergencies Website Feedback Footer middle menu Faculty and Staff Directory Working at Regis Emergency Information Title IX Resources Footer last menu Children's Center Events and Facility Rentals Fine Arts Center ​Administrative Offices 235 Wellesley Street, Weston MA 02493 781.768.7000 © 2026 Privacy Policy Footer social menu TikTok Instagram LinkedIn Facebook Twitter Vimeo YouTube"
